                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                        "entry_id": "1af2ad4a-0623-3247-893f-070948f0959e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A village, or part of a village or parish, in England."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "historical",
                            "obsolete"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "be987bc5-0c58-480b-814b-72bcc1aa6404",
                                "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                                "text": "February 20, 1647, David Greenman and his brother Edward sold to John Greene, husbandman, of Newport, twenty-two acres of land near the endship or village called Greenend, bounded on the southwest side by the road leading from Newport to Portsmouth."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b72f6d9d-71fe-4832-a0a1-f478dcb48e41",
                                "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                                "text": "Twickenham hath two villages or endships belonging to it, which are confidered as a part of its parish."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "a55833ea-da19-47c5-b205-81c1f67f4828",
                                "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                                "text": "In addition to Whitton, Ironside mentions another \"endship\" of the parish of Twickenham called HEATHROW or HETHROW, which must have consisted of a very small collection of houses somewhere on the banks of the Thames, now quite forgotten."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b762a174-ee2c-477a-8f41-27693f945289",
                                "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                                "text": "Whitton Dene was what was known as an endship, that is it was a small suburb or hamlet joined to Whitton, but with its own little community."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "529927da-8f27-42a8-82d2-976c1cc94633",
                                "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                                "text": "It was split between the two townships or endships of Shenley Church End, 673 ha (1662 acres) and Shenley Brook End, 671 ha (1659 acres). The long and straight north-eastern boundary followed the line of Watling Street."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "15ac60b9-a67e-4fe1-a0ee-431e8efefbb1",
                                "sense_id": "eaea9ef7-cdb5-3244-a1a2-4c2902011ab3",
                                "text": "The village emerged from a number of “endships” and the form of the village can be ascribed to \"frontage\" development along the roads linking these “endships”. Between these roads infilling has occurred and there has also been the[…]"
                            }
                        ]
                    }
